## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates understanding of lock-free inter-process communication under simple multicore constraints and cache coherence protocols. It distinguishes candidates who know the mechanics of atomics from those who understand visibility and ordering guarantees.

## Key Concepts
- Lock-free ring buffers with atomic indices
- Acquire/release semantics for safe cross-core visibility
- Wrap-around via power-of-two masking
- Contention handling and backoff strategies

## Code Example
```c
#define SIZE 1024  // power of two
#define MASK (SIZE - 1)

_Atomic size_t head = 0;
_Atomic size_t tail = 0;
type_t buffer[SIZE];

void enqueue(type_t value) {
    size_t h = atomic_fetch_add(&head, 1, memory_order_relaxed);
    buffer[h & MASK] = value;
    atomic_thread_fence(memory_order_release);
}

type_t dequeue(void) {
    size_t t = atomic_load(&tail, memory_order_acquire);
    type_t value = buffer[t & MASK];
    atomic_fetch_add(&tail, 1, memory_order_relaxed);
    return value;
}
```

## Why This Question Is Asked
This question tests the candidate's ability to design a scalable, power-efficient timer subsystem that can handle high-frequency timer events across multiple CPUs while maintaining sub-millisecond wakeup latencies.

## Key Concepts
- **Tickless operation**: Disabling periodic timer ticks during idle periods to reduce power consumption
- **Per-CPU timer buckets**: Local deadline queues that minimize cross-CPU synchronization overhead
- **Global min-heap**: Centralized data structure for tracking the nearest timer expiration across all CPUs
- **Memory ordering**: Using memory barriers to ensure proper visibility of timer state between CPUs
- **Atomic operations**: CAS for lock-free timer insertion and deletion
- **Latency bounds**: Worst-case wakeup latency is determined by hardware interrupt latency plus data structure traversal time

## Code Example
